\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{lie}

\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Lie-algebra representations integrate uniquely on compact Spin}

\p{Let #{n\geq3}, let #{F=\mathbb R} or #{\mathbb C}, and let #{S}
be a finite-dimensional #{F}-vector space. Put #{G=\Spin(Q_n^+)} for
the compact positive-square group of \ref{fcap-0039}, and write
#{b:\mathfrak{so}(n)\to T_1G} for the quadratic lift of
\ref{fcap-0037}. Every real-linear Lie-algebra homomorphism

##{r:\mathfrak{so}(n)\longrightarrow\mathfrak{gl}_F(S)}

has a unique smooth group representation
#{R:G\to GL_F(S)} satisfying

##{dR_1(b(A))=r(A),\qquad
R\bigl(\exp(t b(A))\bigr)=\exp(t r(A)).}

Here #{A\in\mathfrak{so}(n)} and #{t\in\mathbb R}. When
#{F=\mathbb C}, the target #{\mathfrak{gl}_{\mathbb C}(S)} is
regarded as a real Lie algebra: the group being represented is compact real
Spin, and its operators on #{S} are complex-linear.}

\p{\citet{Section 8.3, Proposition 8.41 and Second Principle,
pp. 118--119}{fulton2004representation} proves integration by the
graph-subgroup construction. The concrete Lie structure in
\ref{fcap-0037} and simple connectedness in \ref{fcap-003A} supply
its hypotheses for the selected Spin carrier.}

\subtree{
\taxon{Proof}
\p{Compose #{r} with #{b^{-1}} to obtain a Lie homomorphism
#{\widetilde r:T_1G\to\mathfrak{gl}_F(S)}. Its graph

##{\mathfrak j=\{(X,\widetilde r(X)):X\in T_1G\}}

is a real Lie subalgebra of #{T_1G\oplus\mathfrak{gl}_F(S)}.
The Lie-subgroup theorem integrates it to a connected immersed subgroup
#{J\subseteq G\times GL_F(S)}. Projection onto the first factor
gives a homomorphism #{p:J\to G} whose differential is an isomorphism.}

\p{Thus #{p} is a local diffeomorphism. Its image is an open subgroup
of connected #{G}, hence all of #{G}. Moreover, #{p} is a covering:
choose an identity neighborhood #{U\subseteq J} on which #{p} is a
diffeomorphism onto #{V\subseteq G}. The inverse image of #{V} is the
disjoint union of the translates #{kU}, #{k\in\ker p}; every translate
maps diffeomorphically to #{V}. Since #{J} is connected and #{G} is
simply connected, this covering is an isomorphism. Projection onto the
second factor after #{p^{-1}} is the required #{R}.}

\p{A smooth homomorphism carries a one-parameter subgroup to the
one-parameter subgroup with the differentiated generator. This gives the
exponential identity above. If two homomorphisms have the stated
differential, they therefore agree on the image of an exponential
neighborhood of zero. An identity neighborhood generates every connected
Lie group, so they agree on all of #{G}.}
}

\p{TauCeti's \leanref/tauceti{lieMap_injective} proves this uniqueness
principle for smooth homomorphisms out of a preconnected finite-dimensional
real Lie group. It does not supply the existence construction above. The
restriction #{n\geq3} enters through simple connectedness; the circle
#{\Spin(2)} does not have the same unrestricted integration property.}
